<h2>Why Drainage Matters for Bell County Homes</h2>
<h3>Expansive clay soil and foundations</h3>
<p>Much of the Temple area sits on expansive clay soil that swells when wet and shrinks as it dries. When gutters overflow or downspouts dump water right at the slab, one side of the foundation can become much wetter than the rest. Uneven moisture is a well-known contributor to foundation movement in Central Texas, which can show up as cracked drywall, sticking doors or gaps around windows. Directing roof water several feet away from the house, and keeping soil moisture around the foundation more consistent, is one of the simplest ways to reduce that risk.</p>
<h3>Fascia and roof-edge rot</h3>
<p>When gutters back up, water sits against the fascia board and can wick under the roof edge. Over time the wood softens, the gutter loses its grip, and rot can reach the roof decking. A properly installed drip edge directs water off the roof and into the gutter rather than letting it curl back onto wood. Many older homes in central Temple were roofed before drip edge was routinely used, and we often add it during repairs or <a href="/services/roof-replacement-temple-tx/">roof replacement</a>.</p>

<h2>Gutter Sizing and Downspouts</h2>
<p>Gutter size should match the amount of roof area draining into it, the roof pitch, and the intensity of local rainfall. Steeper roofs send water into the gutter faster. Valleys concentrate flow into a small section of gutter. Our <a href="/tools/roof-pitch-calculator/">roof pitch calculator</a> helps you understand how steep your roof is.</p>
